What is Wire Wedge Bonding?
Wire Wedge Bonding is a foundational semiconductor assembly technique used to create electrical interconnections between an integrated circuit (IC) chip and its packaging. This method is particularly well-suited for applications requiring low profile or fine pitch interconnects. It is also highly effective for creating running stitch interconnects (also known as die-to-die bonding and chain bonding), reverse bonding, and ribbon bonding. The process involves deforming a wire into a wedge shape using a combination of downward force, ultrasonic energy, and, in some cases, heat. The inherent characteristics of wedge bonding make it indispensable for devices where space constraints are paramount, offering superior reliability for specific material combinations and high-current applications.
